.md
# Interactive evaluation and granular updates

## Manipulate and Animate Functionality
WLJS Notebook partially inherits a well-known killer feature of Wolfram Mathematica - Manipulate, Animate, and others, allowing interactive evaluation of arbitrary expressions:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
# Interactive evaluation and granular updates

## Manipulate and Animate Functionality
WLJS Notebook partially inherits a well-known killer feature of Wolfram Mathematica - Manipulate, Animate, and others, allowing interactive evaluation of arbitrary expressions:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Manipulate[
  Plot[Sin[a x + b], {x, 0, 6}], 
  {{a, 2, "Frequency"}, 1, 4, 1}, 
  {{b, 0, "Phase"}, 0, 10, 1}, 
  ContinuousAction->True
]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Manipulate[Plot[1.0 + Sin[w] Sin[x + w],{x,0,5Pi}, Epilog->{
  Red, Point[{8.0, 1.0 + Sin[w] Sin[8.0 + w]}]
}], {w,0,Pi}, ContinuousAction->True]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
Text strings or any other symbolic expressions can also be manipulated:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Text strings or any other symbolic expressions can also be manipulated:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Manipulate[Column[{
  Style[StringTemplate["`` m/s"][v], Blue],
  Table["🚗", {i, Floor[v/25]}] // Row 
}], {v,10,100}, ContinuousAction->True]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
Basic 2D/3D primitives can also be manipulated, for example:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Basic 2D/3D primitives can also be manipulated, for example:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Manipulate[Graphics[{
  Translate[Rotate[Rectangle[q{-1,-1}, q{1,1}], r], {x, 0}]
}, PlotRange->1], {x,-1,1}, {r,0,Pi/2.0}, {q,0.1,0.2}, ContinuousAction->True]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
3D plot example:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
3D plot example:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Manipulate[Plot3D[Sin[n x] Cos[n y], {x,-1,1}, {y,-1,1}], {n, 1, 5, 0.3}, ContinuousAction->True]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
Images:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Images:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
img = ImageResize[ExampleData[ExampleData["TestImage"] // Last], 350];
Manipulate[
  ImageAdjust[img, {c,a}], 
  
  {{c, 0},0,5,0.1}, 
  {{a, 0},0,5,0.1},
  ContinuousAction->True
]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
Under the hood, it uses a diff algorithm to interpret changes between old and new expressions evaluated using corresponding parameters. Then, if possible, the expression is transformed into a more optimized one that uses granular updates instead of full re-evaluation.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Under the hood, it uses a diff algorithm to interpret changes between old and new expressions evaluated using corresponding parameters. Then, if possible, the expression is transformed into a more optimized one that uses granular updates instead of full re-evaluation.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
`Animate` works in a similar way, but scans the parameter automatically:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
`Animate` works in a similar way, but scans the parameter automatically:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Animate[
  ParametricPlot[ReIm @ Exp[-I (\[Phi] + \[Gamma] I \[Phi])], {\[Phi],0,5Pi}, 
    PlotLabel->StringTemplate["\[Gamma] = ``"][\[Gamma]]
  ]
, {\[Gamma],0,0.5}]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
For symbolic data it works too:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
For symbolic data it works too:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Animate[{x, (*SpB[*)Power[x(*|*),(*|*)2](*]SpB*)} // NumberForm, {x,0,10}]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Animate[Row[{Sin[x], "==", Series[Sin[x], {x,0,n}], Invisible[1/2]}], {n, 1, 10, 1}, RefreshRate->3]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
### How to export animation

:::warning
This feature is available only for WLJS desktop application
:::

If you want to export it as GIF, render it to `AnimatedImage` first. For example:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
### How to export animation

:::warning
This feature is available only for WLJS desktop application
:::

If you want to export it as GIF, render it to `AnimatedImage` first. For example:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
w3D[x_,y_,t_] := Total@Table[With[{kx = 1.0 \[Omega], ky = 1.0 \[Omega]}, 
  Cos[ kx x + ky y - \[Omega] t]
], {\[Omega], 0, 3, 1.0}];

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
a = AnimatedImage @ Animate[Plot3D[w3D[x,y,t], {x,-Pi,Pi}, {y,-Pi,Pi}, MaxRecursion->1, Mesh->None, PlotRange->Full], {t,0,5}];

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
Now we can export it as GIF or as a movie:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Now we can export it as GIF or as a movie:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Export["animation.gif", a]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
### More specialized functions
We recommend using `ManipulatePlot` and `ManipulateParametricPlot` for simple interactive curve plotting, since they are more optimized and do not require the diff algorithm to run:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
### More specialized functions
We recommend using `ManipulatePlot` and `ManipulateParametricPlot` for simple interactive curve plotting, since they are more optimized and do not require the diff algorithm to run:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
ManipulatePlot[
  (*TB[*)Sum[(*|*)(*FB[*)((Sin[2π(2j - 1) x])(*,*)/(*,*)(2j-1))(*]FB*)(*|*), {(*|*)j(*|*),(*|*)1.0(*|*),(*|*)n(*|*)}](*|*)(*1:eJxTTMoPSmNiYGAoZgMSwaW5TvkVmYwgPguQCCkqTQUAeAcHBQ==*)(*]TB*)
, {x, -1,1}, {{n,4}, 1,7, 1}]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
`AnimatePlot` and `AnimateParametricPlot` use a different strategy and precalculate all curves before animating:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
`AnimatePlot` and `AnimateParametricPlot` use a different strategy and precalculate all curves before animating:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
AnimateParametricPlot[
    With[{t = If[x > lim,lim,x]},
      N[Exp[-(*FB[*)((1)(*,*)/(*,*)(3))(*]FB*) Floor[(*FB[*)((x)(*,*)/(*,*)(4 \[Pi]))(*]FB*)]] ReIm[(*SpB[*)Power[I(*|*),(*|*)-t](*]SpB*) + 3 ((*SpB[*)Power[I(*|*),(*|*)t/3](*]SpB*))]]
    ]
    , {x, 0.1`, 16 \[Pi]}, {lim, 0.1`, 16 \[Pi]}
]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
(*VB[*)(FrontEndRef["Fdfc25f53-fe4f-455e-ad0c-baff9ca2ad41"])(*,*)(*"1:eJxTTMoPSmNkYGAoZgESHvk5KRCeEJBwK8rPK3HNS3GtSE0uLUlMykkNVgUJp6QlG5mmmRrrpqWapOmamJqm6iamGCTrJiWmpVkmJxolppgYAgCyFRc0"*)(*]VB*)

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
### Tips for better performance
If you still want to use `Animate` or `Manipulate` on some complex expressions, there are some tricks to avoid deoptimization of JIT engine:

<div class="text-sm mt-2">


- Try to keep the number of curves/traces the same as well as polygons if possible. Try not to add new entities to your graphs.

- `PerformanceGoal->"Speed"` is also a good option for 3D plots.

- `Mesh->None might` also help.

- `PlotRange->Full` can help to avoid fragmentation of line segments

- Avoid changing colors, opacity on runtime

  
</div>

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
### Tips for better performance
If you still want to use `Animate` or `Manipulate` on some complex expressions, there are some tricks to avoid deoptimization of JIT engine:

<div class="text-sm mt-2">


- Try to keep the number of curves/traces the same as well as polygons if possible. Try not to add new entities to your graphs.

- `PerformanceGoal->"Speed"` is also a good option for 3D plots.

- `Mesh->None might` also help.

- `PlotRange->Full` can help to avoid fragmentation of line segments

- Avoid changing colors, opacity on runtime

  
</div>

